When converting a std::string to a double using std::stod, if the value in the string is outside the representable range for a double (e.g., an extremely large or small exponent), std::stod will throw a std::out_of_range exception. If the string does not represent a valid floating-point number, it throws a std::invalid_argument exception.

Here’s a sample way to check if the string is out of bounds of double:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>

bool isOutOfDoubleBounds(const std::string& input) {
    try {
        size_t pos = 0;
        double val = std::stod(input, &pos);
        // Optionally, check if the entire string was parsed
        if (pos != input.size()) return true;
        // Optional: check for infinity explicitly
        if (!std::isfinite(val)) return true;
        return false;
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
        // Not a valid number
        return true;
    } catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
        // Out of double bounds
        return true;
    }
}
```

This code tries to convert the string. If an exception is thrown, it’s either not a number at all or it’s out of double’s bounds. We also check if all characters are consumed for a stricter check, and you could also check with std::isfinite to rule out infinity and nan, which may indicate out-of-bound inputs (but these are representable as double).

You could also use C functions like strtod if you prefer errno-based error checking, or (if you're using C++17 or later) std::from_chars, which returns error codes you can check.

---

