To check if the number represented by a string is out of bounds for a `double` in C++, the most common approach is to use `std::stod`, and handle the exceptions it may throw. Specifically:

- If the string is not valid as a number, it will throw `std::invalid_argument`.
- If the value is outside what can be represented by a `double` (e.g., too large), it will throw `std::out_of_range`.

Here’s a simple function that returns true if the value in the string is out of bounds for a `double`:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>

bool isOutOfDoubleBounds(const std::string& s) {
    try {
        size_t idx;
        double val = std::stod(s, &idx);
        // Check that the whole string was parsed (no trailing non-number characters)
        if (idx != s.size()) {
            return true; // Invalid/extra characters
        }
        if (!std::isfinite(val)) {
            return true; // Result is infinity or NaN
        }
        return false; // Valid double within bounds
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
        return true;
    } catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
        return true;
    }
}
```

This function:
- Returns true if the value is out of the representable range for double,
- Or if the string is not a valid (entirely) number,
- Or if the conversion results in infinity/NaN.

You may also consider using `std::strtod` for more detailed error handling, particularly if you want to avoid exceptions and check `errno`.

