To implement automatic garbage collection for a linked list in C++, you should leverage RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) using smart pointers. The typical approach is to manage each node's next pointer as a `std::unique_ptr`, making node ownership explicit and ensuring that when a node is destroyed, it recursively deletes (garbage collects) the following nodes.

Here is an example implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Node {
    int data;
    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;
    Node(int value) : data(value), next(nullptr) {}
};

class LinkedList {
    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;
public:
    void push_front(int value) {
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>(value);
        new_node->next = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(new_node);
    }

    void print() const {
        Node* curr = head.get();
        while (curr) {
            std::cout << curr->data << " -> ";
            curr = curr->next.get();
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;
    list.push_front(1);
    list.push_front(2);
    list.push_front(3);
    list.print(); // 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> nullptr
    // When list goes out of scope, memory is automatically freed.
}
```

Explanation:
- `std::unique_ptr` automatically deletes the nodes recursively when the owning node or list object is destroyed.
- This is the "automatic garbage collection" for singly linked lists in modern C++.

