```java
@Bean
@Order(1)
public SecurityFilterChain firstRefererChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    RequestHeaderRequestMatcher matcher = new RequestHeaderRequestMatcher("Referer", "https://foo.com");
    http.securityMatcher(matcher)
        .addFilterBefore(new FooRefererFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
@Order(2)
public SecurityFilterChain secondRefererChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    RequestHeaderRequestMatcher matcher = new RequestHeaderRequestMatcher("Referer", "https://bar.com");
    http.securityMatcher(matcher)
        .addFilterBefore(new BarRefererFilter(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
@Order(3)
public SecurityFilterChain defaultChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().permitAll());
    return http.build();
}
```

3. **Custom Matchers for Complex Logic**:  
If you need to match on partial values or patterns in the "Referer" header, write a custom `RequestMatcher`:

```java
public class CustomRefererMatcher implements RequestMatcher {
    @Override
    public boolean matches(HttpServletRequest request) {
        String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");
        // insert your logic, ex: contains, startsWith, regex, etc.
        return referer != null && referer.contains("somePattern");
    }
}
```

4. **Security Consideration**:  
The "Referer" header is controlled by the client and can be spoofed. Don’t rely on this for critical security decisions.

5. **Custom Filter Example**:  
```java
public class FooRefererFilter extends GenericFilterBean {
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        // custom filtering logic
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
```

**Summary**:  
Use separate `SecurityFilterChain` beans with custom (or built-in) `RequestMatcher`s for each "Referer" value. Insert your custom filters into each chain as needed. Spring Security will automatically route requests to the correct chain based on the matcher.

