To implement a fast `strlen` in C when `size_t` is exactly the size of a word, the common approach is:

1.  Traverse the string byte-by-byte until the pointer is aligned to a `size_t` (word) boundary.
2.  Check word-sized chunks (i.e., `size_t` at a time), using a bit-trick to quickly detect whether any byte in the word is zero.
3.  When a word is found that might contain a zero byte, switch back to byte-wise scan to pinpoint the null terminator.

Here's how it can look:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <limits.h>

size_t fast_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *start = s;

    // Process bytes until s is size_t aligned
    while ((uintptr_t)s % sizeof(size_t)) {
        if (*s == '\0')
            return s - start;
        s++;
    }

    // Now s is word-aligned
    const size_t *wptr = (const size_t *)s;
#if SIZE_MAX == 0xffffffffUL
    const size_t ONES = 0x01010101UL;
    const size_t HIGHS = 0x80808080UL;
#elif SIZE_MAX == 0xffffffffffffffffUL
    const size_t ONES = 0x0101010101010101UL;
    const size_t HIGHS = 0x8080808080808080UL;
#else
#error Unsupported size_t size
#endif

    while (1) {
        size_t word = *wptr;
        if (((word - ONES) & ~word & HIGHS) != 0) {
            // There is at least one zero byte in word
            s = (const char *)wptr;
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(size_t); i++) {
                if (s[i] == '\0')
                    return s + i - start;
            }
        }
        wptr++;
    }
}
```

### Explanation
- The key bit-trick is `((word - ONES) & ~word & HIGHS)`. This expression will be nonzero if any byte in `word` is zero.
- The function first advances until the string pointer is word-aligned to avoid unaligned memory access issues.
- After finding a word with a zero byte, it searches byte-by-byte for the null terminator to return the correct length.

This approach is portable for both 32-bit and 64-bit `size_t`.

