## My Own Answer

**Task Recap:**  
Given an Excel file and a cell address, check using C++, Qt, and QXlsx if the cell contains *exactly* one of the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 11, or 42 — _regardless_ if represented as int, float, or as a string in hex (e.g. "0x2A"), octal, binary (e.g. "0b1010"), or decimal.

**Solution Overview:**  
1. Open the file, get the cell value as QVariant.
2. If the value is numeric (double, int, etc.), check if it's an integer (no fractional part), then compare.
3. If the value is a string, try to parse it in:
    - hex (starts with 0x or 0X)
    - binary (starts with 0b or 0B)
    - octal (starts with 0 and longer than 1 char)
    - decimal (fallback)
    - Also try to parse as a double, then check if it is integer
4. Only accept if the integer value matches the allowed set.

```cpp
#include <QCoreApplication>
#include <QDebug>
#include <QVariant>
#include <QSet>
#include "xlsxdocument.h"

// Helper function to parse string input into integer (hex, bin, oct, dec, float->int)
bool parseStringNumber(const QString &str, int &out)
{
    QString s = str.trimmed();
    if (s.isEmpty()) return false;

    bool ok = false;

    // Hexadecimal
    if (s.startsWith("0x", Qt::CaseInsensitive)) {
        out = s.mid(2).toInt(&ok, 16);
        if (ok) return true;
    }
    // Binary
    else if (s.startsWith("0b", Qt::CaseInsensitive)) {
        out = s.mid(2).toInt(&ok, 2);
        if (ok) return true;
    }
    // Octal (starts with '0' but isn't hex or bin)
    else if (s.startsWith('0') && s.length() > 1) {
        out = s.toInt(&ok, 8);
        if (ok) return true;
    }
    // Decimal integer
    out = s.toInt(&ok, 10);
    if (ok) return true;

    // Check if it's a float that can be represented as int (e.g., "3.0")
    double asDouble = s.toDouble(&ok);
    if (ok && qFuzzyCompare(asDouble + 1, static_cast<int>(asDouble) + 1)) {
        out = static_cast<int>(asDouble);
        return true;
    }

    return false;
}

bool cellHasAllowedValue(const QVariant &cellValue)
{
    QSet<int> allowed = {0, 1, 2, 3, 10, 11, 42};
    int val = 0;
    bool ok = false;

    // If cell is numeric
    if (cellValue.canConvert<double>()) {
        double d = cellValue.toDouble();
        if (qFuzzyCompare(d + 1, static_cast<int>(d) + 1)) {
            val = static_cast<int>(d);
            ok = true;
        }
    }
    // If cell is string
    else if (cellValue.type() == QVariant::String) {
        ok = parseStringNumber(cellValue.toString(), val);
    }

    return ok && allowed.contains(val);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    QCoreApplication app(argc, argv);

    QString xlsxPath = "example.xlsx";
    QString cellRef = "A1";
    QXlsx::Document doc(xlsxPath);

    if (!doc.load()) {
        qWarning() << "Failed to load xlsx";
        return 1;
    }
    QVariant cellVal = doc.read(cellRef);
    if (!cellVal.isValid()) {
        qWarning() << "Invalid cell";
        return 2;
    }

    if (cellHasAllowedValue(cellVal)) {
        qInfo() << "Cell" << cellRef << "contains allowed value";
    } else {
        qInfo() << "Cell" << cellRef << "does NOT contain allowed value";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Notes:**  
- Covers all bases/types/formats.
- The helper function is robust: tries int parsing (multi-base), then float-to-int.
- Uses QSet for allowed values.
- Proper handling if the cell is blank or invalid.

